' APS Substrate Dispatch Loop (RFC-0037). Turns "an interrupt happened" (RFC-0036's tick, and
' every future device interrupt sharing the same top-half/bottom-half shape) into "a registered
' Provider's hook ran" -- on a single logical processor, cooperatively, deterministically, without
' a hook or its caller ever needing to remember EOI or re-enabling interrupts by hand.

#PROFILE UEFI
#TARGET X86_64
#RUNTIME NONE

' --- A documented deviation from RFC-0037 Section 6.3's literal signature, stated up front. ---
'
' RFC-0037 specifies `Loop.RegisterHook(eventSource AS U32, hook AS CALLABLE) AS BOOL`. This
' implementation instead uses `Loop.RegisterHook(eventSource AS U32, hookSlot AS U32) AS BOOL`.
'
' Why: RFC-0027 (First-Class Callables) defines `ADDRESSOF Name` / `AS CALLABLE` / indirect
' invocation, but none of that machinery has freestanding-x86-64 codegen support today --
' confirmed directly: `ADDRESSOF Name` parses and lowers to a real A-MIR AddressOf instruction
' (the frontend/A-MIR layers are RFC-0027-complete), but `generate_x86_64_function`
' (src/compiler/fission.cpp) has no case for AmirInstruction::Kind::AddressOf at all, and
' AmirInstruction::Kind::CallValue's freestanding codegen unconditionally resolves its target as a
' compile-time-known *function name string* looked up against the module's declared functions --
' it cannot call through a value loaded from a variable/register. This is exactly the gap RFC-0037
' Section 17.5 names as a stop condition, with an explicit instruction: "fall back to the
' function-pointer-table shape sketched in Section 15 ... rather than expanding this RFC's scope
' to fix RFC-0027's freestanding coverage inline." Building genuine indirect invocation (a new
' AMIR kind, new codegen using the encoder's existing but currently UEFI-vtable-only
' call_indirect_disp8/32 primitives, target-resolution logic distinguishing a local CALLABLE
' variable from a declared function name) is real, separate compiler work belonging to RFC-0027's
' own freestanding-profile completion, not to this RFC.
'
' The fallback actually implemented: `LoopInvokeSlot` (below) is Section 15's own
' `InvokeHookAt(index)` sketch, made concrete as a fixed-size (kLoopMaxHooks = 4) IF-chain over a
' compile-time-bounded slot index, dispatching to one of a small, fixed set of well-known function
' names (`LoopHookSlot0`..`LoopHookSlot3`) that the *consuming program* defines -- registration
' (which event source maps to which slot, and whether that mapping is currently active) remains
' fully dynamic and runtime-driven, exactly as RFC-0037 Requirement 6.3 specifies; only the set of
' possible hook *bodies* is fixed per compiled program rather than arbitrary at runtime. Every
' other requirement in this RFC (Loop structure, top-half/bottom-half discipline, dispatch
' ordering, replace-on-reregister, Run/RunUntil) is implemented exactly as specified, with no
' further deviation.

' --- Hook registration table: kLoopMaxHooks rows of (eventSource, hookSlot, active), 24 bytes
' each, at a fixed scratch address (policy-owned, same pattern as timer_policy.abas's own
' TimerFrequencyScratchAddress -- see that file's header for why a small fixed address is this
' codebase's established way to persist state the freestanding profile's lack of a general
' global-variable mechanism can't otherwise hold). Deliberately clear of every other scratch
' address this project has claimed so far (0x2000000, 0x2010000..0x2010080, 0x2018000).
FUNCTION LoopHookTableAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(33685504))   ' 0x2020000
END FUNCTION

' Dispatch cursor: one U64 per possible event source (RFC-0036's 16 IRQ lines), recording the
' interrupt-pending-table count last observed for that source. RFC-0036's pending table
' (CPU.InterruptPendingTableBase()) is a monotonic counter that Timer.Ticks() also reads directly
' and never clears (RFC-0036 Requirement 6.4 left "counter vs flag" to implementation; that choice
' was made specifically so direct polling of Timer.Ticks() keeps working). Requirement 6.2's "clear
' the pending indication before invoking the hook" is therefore implemented here as "advance this
' Loop's own high-water mark for that source" rather than mutating the shared RFC-0036 table --
' mutating a table a second, independent consumer (Timer.Ticks()) also reads would make the two
' consumers race each other for no benefit; a private cursor gives the Loop exactly the
' clear-before-invoke, re-arrival-not-lost semantics Requirement 6.2 asks for without disturbing
' RFC-0036's own contract at all.
FUNCTION LoopDispatchCursorAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(33689600))   ' 0x2021000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION LoopMaxHooks() AS U32
    RETURN 4
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION LoopMaxEventSources() AS U32
    RETURN 16   ' RFC-0036's IRQ0-15 range; this implementation's event-source space
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION LoopHookRowAddress(row AS U32) AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(LoopHookTableAddress(), row * 24)
END FUNCTION

' --- Registration ---

' Requirement 6.3: replaces an existing hook for the same event source rather than allocating a
' second row for it; claims a free row only when no row is already registered for this source;
' returns FALSE if the table is full and no existing row can be reused.
FUNCTION Loop.RegisterHook(eventSource AS U32, hookSlot AS U32) AS BOOL
    LET row AS U32 = 0
    LET existingRow AS U32 = 4294967295
    LET freeRow AS U32 = 4294967295
    WHILE row < LoopMaxHooks()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = LoopHookRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16))
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET source AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
            IF source = eventSource THEN
                existingRow = row
            END IF
        ELSE
            IF freeRow = 4294967295 THEN
                freeRow = row
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND

    LET targetRow AS U32 = existingRow
    IF targetRow = 4294967295 THEN
        targetRow = freeRow
    END IF
    IF targetRow = 4294967295 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET target AS MMIOPTR = LoopHookRowAddress(targetRow)
    MEMORY.Write64(target, eventSource)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(target, 8), hookSlot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(target, 16), 1)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION Loop.UnregisterHook(eventSource AS U32) AS BOOL
    LET row AS U32 = 0
    WHILE row < LoopMaxHooks()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = LoopHookRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16))
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET source AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
            IF source = eventSource THEN
                MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), 0)
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Returns TRUE and writes the registered slot into slotOut if eventSource has an active
' registration; returns FALSE (slotOut left unwritten) otherwise.
FUNCTION LoopFindHook(eventSource AS U32, slotOutAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    LET row AS U32 = 0
    WHILE row < LoopMaxHooks()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = LoopHookRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16))
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET source AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
            IF source = eventSource THEN
                LET slot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8))
                MEMORY.Write64(slotOutAddress, slot)
                RETURN 1
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' --- Dispatch (bottom half; ordinary code running with interrupts enabled, per Requirement 6.2 --
' never called from an ISR) ---

' Section 15's InvokeHookAt(index), made concrete as the fixed-slot IF-chain this file's header
' comment explains. A consuming program supplies LoopHookSlot0..LoopHookSlot3; slots this program
' does not define/use are simply never registered against any event source.
FUNCTION LoopInvokeSlot(slot AS U32) AS U64
    IF slot = 0 THEN RETURN LoopHookSlot0()
    IF slot = 1 THEN RETURN LoopHookSlot1()
    IF slot = 2 THEN RETURN LoopHookSlot2()
    IF slot = 3 THEN RETURN LoopHookSlot3()
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' For each possible event source, in ascending order (Requirement 6.3's deterministic tie-break --
' iterating the source space directly, rather than sorting registration-order rows, gets this for
' free): if it has an active registration AND the RFC-0036 pending-table count has advanced past
' this Loop's own last-observed cursor for it, advance the cursor *before* invoking the hook (so a
' re-arrival during the hook's own execution is observed on the Loop's next wake, not lost), then
' invoke it.
FUNCTION LoopDispatchPendingWork() AS U64
    LET pendingTable AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(CPU.InterruptPendingTableBase()))
    LET cursorTable AS MMIOPTR = LoopDispatchCursorAddress()
    LET slotOut AS U64 = 0
    LET slotOutAddress AS PTR = ADDRESS.Local(slotOut)

    LET source AS U32 = 0
    WHILE source < LoopMaxEventSources()
        IF LoopFindHook(source, slotOutAddress) = 1 THEN
            LET currentCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(pendingTable, source * 8))
            LET lastSeen AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(cursorTable, source * 8))
            IF currentCount <> lastSeen THEN
                MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(cursorTable, source * 8), currentCount)
                LoopInvokeSlot(slotOut AND 4294967295)
            END IF
        END IF
        source = source + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' --- Requirement 6.1: the Loop itself. CPU.EnableInterrupts is called exactly once, as the first
' action of whichever entry point actually runs (production callers use Loop.Run(); tests use
' Loop.RunUntil()) -- never both, since a real program calls exactly one of the two. ---

FUNCTION Loop.RunUntil(maxWakes AS U64) AS U64
    CPU.EnableInterrupts
    LET wakes AS U64 = 0
    WHILE wakes < maxWakes
        CPU.Halt
        LoopDispatchPendingWork()
        wakes = wakes + 1
    WEND
    RETURN wakes
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION Loop.Run() AS U64
    CPU.EnableInterrupts
    WHILE 1 = 1
        CPU.Halt
        LoopDispatchPendingWork()
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION
